            "question": "Explain the Micrometer Screw Gauge in detail: construction, pitch, least count derivation, zero error determination, and experimental steps to measure wire diameter.",
            "answer": "1. Construction: Consists of a U-shaped metallic frame with a fixed anvil on one end and a movable spindle connected to a threaded screw inside a cylindrical sleeve (main scale) and thimble (circular scale). A ratchet mechanism prevents excessive pressure on the object.\n2. Pitch and Least Count: Pitch is the linear distance traversed along the main scale in one complete turn of the thimble ($1\\text{ mm}$ or $0.5\\text{ mm}$). Least Count = $\\text{Pitch} / \\text{Total Circular Scale Divisions} = 1\\text{ mm} / 100 = 0.01\\text{ mm} = 0.001\\text{ cm}$.\n3. Zero Error: When anvil and spindle touch: if 0 of circular scale coincides with index line, zero error is zero. If circular 0 is below index line, error is positive (subtract). If circular 0 is above index line, error is negative (add).\n4. Procedure: Place the wire between anvil and spindle. Turn ratchet until it clicks three times. Read the main scale reading (MSR) and the coinciding circular division (CSR). Calculate $\\text{Total Diameter} = \\text{MSR} + (\\text{CSR} \\times \\text{LC}) - (\\pm \\text{Zero Error})$.",
