            "question": "State and prove the Law of Conservation of Momentum for an isolated system of two colliding spheres, and explain its application to rocket propulsion.",
            "answer": "1. Statement: 'The total linear momentum of an isolated system of interacting bodies remains constant if no external unbalanced force acts on it.'\n\n2. Derivation:\nConsider two spheres of masses $m_1$ and $m_2$ moving in a straight line with initial velocities $u_1$ and $u_2$ ($u_1 > u_2$).\n- Total initial momentum before collision: $P_i = m_1 u_1 + m_2 u_2$.\n- The spheres collide for time $t$. Let $F_1$ be the force exerted by $m_1$ on $m_2$, and $F_2$ be the reaction force exerted by $m_2$ on $m_1$.\n- By Newton's Third Law: $F_1 = -F_2$.\n- By Newton's Second Law in momentum form:\n  $F_1 = \\frac{m_2 v_2 - m_2 u_2}{t}$ and $F_2 = \\frac{m_1 v_1 - m_1 u_1}{t}$.\n- Substituting into $F_1 = -F_2$:\n  $\\frac{m_2 v_2 - m_2 u_2}{t} = -\\frac{m_1 v_1 - m_1 u_1}{t}$.\n  $m_2 v_2 - m_2 u_2 = -m_1 v_1 + m_1 u_1$.\n- Rearranging terms:\n  $m_1 u_1 + m_2 u_2 = m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2$.\n  $\\text{Total Initial Momentum} = \\text{Total Final Momentum}$. Proved.\n\n3. Application to Rocket Propulsion:\nA rocket carries fuel and liquid oxidizer. When combustion occurs, hot gases are ejected backward at supersonic speeds carrying massive backward momentum. By conservation of momentum, the rocket body acquires an equal forward momentum, propelling it into space.",
