          "text": "6.3 KINETIC ENERGY\n\nenergy.\n\nThe energy possessed by a body due to its motion is called Kinetic\n\nWhen a cricket ball is thrown it moves, similarly a car starts moving when pushed. Now Think that a cricket ball and a car are moving with same speed. Which possess greater ability to do work? Of course it is the car with larger mass, it is difficult to stop. Similarly now two cricket balls are approaching you with different speeds, which can do more work? Again it is easy to answer as the ball with greater speed is difficult to stop. Thus object mass and its speed are contributing to its kinetic energy.\n\nMathematically Kinetic energy is one half the product of an object's mass 'm' and the square of its velocity 'v'.\n\n1 Ex = m\n\nmv2 2\n\nPOINT TO PONDER\n\nDOES THE EARTH DO WORK ON THE MOON?\n\nThe Moon revolves around the Earth in a nearly circular orbit, kept there by the gravitational force exerted by the Earth. This gravitational force provides it centripetal acceleration, inward along the radius of the Moon's orbit. The Moon's displacement at any moment is tangent to the circle, perpendicular to the force of gravity F.. Hence the angle between the force and the instantaneous ! displacement of the Moon is 90°, and the work done by gravity is therefore zero. (W= F S cos 90°= 0). This is why the Moon, as well as artificial satellites, can stay in orbit without expenditure of fuel: no work needs to be done\n\nagainst the force of gravity.\n\nmoon\n\nEarth\n\nF1\n\nWork and Energy\n\nType\n\nTABLE 6.1: TYPES OF ENERGY\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nThese bonds can take many different\n\nThe energy contained within the forms, including energy derived from\n\nChemical Energy\n\nbonds between atoms.\n\nRadiant Energy\n\nElectrical Energy\n\nHeat Enrgy\n\nSound Energy\n\nNuclear Energy\n\ncarbohydrates in food to energy stored in\n\ngasoline.\n\nRadiant energy from the Sun supplies Earth Radiant energy travels as an with all of the energy required to sustain\n\nelectromagnetic (light) wave.\n\nlife.\n\nThe energy associated with Electrons moving from negatively to\n\ncharges.\n\nEnergy that travel from hot body to cold body.\n\nEnergy as wave, as a sound wave passes through, the atoms or molecules of the substance vibrate back and forth.\n\npositively charged objects.\n\nFire burning transfer the energy to keep\n\nroom warm.\n\n909\n\nSound vibrations cause a person's eardrums to vibrate.\n\nMed 19 no s nad\n\nEnergy in the Nucleus of an Nuclear power stations use nuclear atom.\n\nto generate electric energy.\n\n916\n\nLike all energies kinetic Figure 6.33 bin\n\nenergy is also a scalar quantity. For example, a 2.0 kg hammer head moving with a speed of 4.0 m/s has a kinetic energy of 16 J. Mathematical proof:\n\nConsider a situation in which all of the work done on a cart transfers only kinetic energy to the\n\nThe moving\n\nhammer has\n\nv= 4.0 m/s\n\nkinetic energy bends decisUSM and thus can solv\n\nm= 2.0 kg do work on the\n\nnail, driving it into the wall.\n\nenergy\n\ncart. Consider a cart which is initially at rest. A horizontal force F is applied to it comes it to move through a displacement 's' and achieve a final velocity of v,=v as shown in figure 6.4. This work done W appears as the kinetic energy k,. Such\n\nFigure 6.4 Work\n\nthat\n\nW = Ex = FxS\n\nF\n\n1\n\nBy Newton's Second Law of motion\n\nF = ma\n\n2\n\n93\n\nS\n\nWork and Energy\n\nBy third equation of motion\n\n2as = v2 -v2\n\nrearranging\n\nv} − v?\n\nS=\n\n2a\n\n3\n\nputting equation 2 and 3 in equation 1\n\nEx = máx\n\nv2-v2\n\nINFORMATION\n\n2a\n\nv} -v?\n\nor\n\nEx = mx\n\n2\n\nAs the object started from rest therefore v1 = 0 and v1 = v\n\nEx=mv2 mv2\n\n6.3\n\nMagnetar: On December 27, 2004, astronomers observed the greatest flash of light ever recorded from outside the solar system. It came from the highly magnetic neutron star SGR 1806- 20 (a magnetar). During 0.20 s, this star released as much energy as our sun does in 250,000\n\nyears.\n\nThis equation shows the relation between the kinetic energy of a moving object with its mass and velocity. Equally important, it demonstrate the work kinetic energy theorem which states that the work done on an object is equal to change in energy.\n\nExample 6.2 BULLET SPEED\n\nA 60.0 g bullet is fired from a gun with 3150 J of\n\nkinetic energy. Find its velocity.\n\nGIVEN:\n\nREQUIRED:\n\n-> Bullet\n\nmass m = 60 g = 0.06 kg\n\nvelocity v = ?\n\nKinetic energy E1 = 3150 J\n\nThe kinetic energy is given as\n\nrearranging\n\n1\n\nEx = n\n\n2\n\nmv2\n\nv2 = 2Ek\n\nm\n\nEXTENSION EXERCISE 6.1 If a car of mass 800 kg is having same kinetic energy, what will be its speed?\n\nWork and Energy\n\ntaking square root on both sides\n\nor\n\nputting values\n\n=\n\n2Ex\n\nV=\n\nV m\n\n2E\n\nm\n\n2 x 3150\n\nkgm2/s2\n\nV =\n\nX\n\n0.06 kg\n\ns\n\nas 1 J = kg m2 s2\n\ntherefore\n\nv = 324 m/s\n\nAnswer\n\nThe bullet is moving with speed 324 m/s.\n\nAssignment 6.2 BULLET KINETIC ENERGY\n\nAbullet of mass 30 g travels at a speed of 400 ms. Calculate its kinetic energy."
