          "text": "7.2 DENSITY\n\nDensity of a substance is defined\n\nas the mass of substance per unit volume. For example iron is denser than air, because iron has more mass in the same volume as compared to air. Conversely for same mass of iron and air, volume of iron will be less than air. Symbolically density is denoted by 'p' (called Rho). For an object of mass 'm' and volume 'V', density is given as\n\nTABLE 7.1 DENSITIES\n\nMaterial\n\niron\n\nDensity (kg/m3)\n\n7900\n\ngold\n\n19300\n\nice\n\n920\n\npolythene\n\n900\n\npetrol\n\n800\n\npure water\n\n1000\n\n13600\n\nmercury\n\nair\n\n1.3\n\n2.0\n\nm\n\n7.1\n\nV\n\ncarbon dioxide\n\nHence the S.I. unit of density is kg m3. Some common density values, are\n\ngiven in table 7.1.\n\nPage\n\n173\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nExample 7.1\n\nDENSITY OF METAL\n\nA rectangular metal piece has length 0.3 m, width 0.2 m and height 0.1 m. If its\n\nmass is 47.4 kg. Calculate its density.\n\nS GIVEN:\n\nO Mass of metal piece m = 47.4 kg\n\nL Volume V = length x width x height\n\nU\n\n= 0.3 m x 0.2 m x 0.1 m = 0.006 m\n\nREQUIRED:\n\ndensity p =?\n\nm\n\nBy definition of density' p =\n\nV\n\n0\n\n47.4kg\n\nputting valties\n\nΡ\n\n0.006 m3\n\nN\n\ntherefore 24th\n\np = 7900 kg m-3\n\nAnswer\n\nFrom the table 7.1 we can see that it is density of iron, therefore the metal piece under study is that of iron.\n\nAssignment 7.1 DENSITY OF DIAMOND\n\nA diamond has a volume of 0.00002 m3, its mass is measured\n\nas 0.072 kg. Calculate its density."
