            "q": "Describe the three modes of heat transfer with examples and practical applications.",
            "ans": "1. Conduction (in solids): Heat transfers by molecular vibrations. Hot molecules vibrate faster and pass energy to neighboring molecules. Conductors: metals (copper, silver). Insulators: wood, plastic, air. Application: Cooking pots (metal bottom, plastic handle). Building walls use brick (poor conductor). 2. Convection (in fluids): Heat transfers by actual movement of fluid particles. Hot fluid expands → less dense → rises; cooler fluid sinks. Examples: Boiling water, sea/land breezes, room heating. Application: Ceiling fans, hot water radiators, sea breeze for coastal cooling. 3. Radiation (in vacuum/air): Heat transfers by infrared electromagnetic waves at speed of light. No medium needed. Black surfaces: best absorbers and emitters. White/polished surfaces: best reflectors. Application: Solar heater uses black surface to absorb radiation. Thermos flask uses silvered walls to reflect radiation. Sun warms Earth through 150 million km of space."
